/*
RMSTEST_28b - CONTROL FOR 28a. Identical except clumping_factor is 0, not -5.
preview-design.md Sec.15 item 13

WHY THIS EXISTS
RMSTEST_21 saw fragmentation at BOTH cf -5 (six pieces) and cf 0 (three). If
only the negative value fragments, the mechanism is specific to negative
clumping and Sec.6.1's growth model survives for the normal range. If 0
fragments too, the boundary is somewhere in the low range and the model is
wrong across a band of ordinary values, which is much more serious - cf 0 is a
value real maps write.

Read the two together. Separately they each show a number of patches; together
they show whether the count depends on the sign or on the magnitude.

Everything else is byte-for-byte 28a: one land, map center, fuzziness off, no
cliff section, same tile budget, same map size.

GENERATE AT NORMAL (200), any player count. Same sitting as 28a.

PREDICTIONS, RECORDED BEFORE THE RUN
1. One SNOW patch, for the same structural reason as 28a.
2. If both fragment, expect FEWER pieces here than at -5 (RMSTEST_21 saw 3
   against 6). A monotone relationship between clumping factor and piece count
   is itself the finding: it would mean the clumping factor is controlling how
   far the growth candidate set reaches, not how tightly it clusters, and
   Sec.6.1's whole reading of the parameter is inverted.
3. If 28a fragments and this does NOT, the effect is sign-specific and the
   search narrows to whatever the engine does with a negative here.

READ IT WITH
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --patches SNOW

RUN IT THREE TIMES, matching 28a run for run.
*/

<PLAYER_SETUP>
  random_placement

<LAND_GENERATION>
  base_terrain GRASS

  create_land
  {
    terrain_type SNOW
    number_of_tiles 400
    base_size 1
    clumping_factor 0
    border_fuzziness 0
    land_position 50 50
  }

<ELEVATION_GENERATION>


<TERRAIN_GENERATION>

<OBJECTS_GENERATION>

<CONNECTION_GENERATION>
